# Test environment

Local-only config consumed by the `tester` agent and the `/lmd:explore` skill.
Copy this file to `<your-project>/.lmd/test-env.md` and adjust to your project.

If absent, runtime UI verification is disabled — every runtime-classified
acceptance criterion will fail with an instruction to create this file.

Format is free-form Markdown — no strict schema. The two examples below
cover the common cases (single-server and multi-server). Pick whichever
fits, or write your own shape as long as the server URLs, login URL, and
named user credentials are unambiguous.

## Test Server

Dev server: http://localhost:5173
Start command: npm run dev   # tester auto-starts if probe fails, then kills on exit

## Test Auth

Method: password        # password (default) | storageState — see the SSO block below

Login URL: http://localhost:5173/login

Test users:
  - default: test@example.com / testpass
  - admin:   admin@example.com / adminpass

Throwaway destroyable user (for delete-account tests):
  - throwaway-pool: see scripts/seed-throwaway.ts

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# SSO / external-IdP auth (Azure AD, Google, Okta, Auth0, Keycloak, …):
#
# When the app redirects login to an EXTERNAL identity provider, tester
# cannot drive the IdP form (different origin, multi-step, often MFA).
# Instead, log in ONCE yourself and hand tester a saved session. Replace
# the `## Test Auth` block above with:
#
# ## Test Auth
#
# Method: storageState
# Storage states:
#   - default: .lmd/auth/default.json
#   - admin:   .lmd/auth/admin.json
#
# Tester loads the JSON into the browser context (cookies + localStorage)
# and SKIPS the login screen entirely — it never touches the IdP.
#
# Create / refresh a state file (do this whenever tester reports the
# session expired):
#   npx playwright codegen --save-storage=.lmd/auth/default.json https://your-app.example.com
# …log in through SSO in the opened browser (complete any MFA), reach an
# authenticated page, then CLOSE the window. The cookies + localStorage
# are written to the JSON.
#
# SECURITY: these files hold LIVE session tokens. NEVER commit them —
# keep `.lmd/auth/` gitignored (the default `.lmd/` rule already covers
# it; do NOT add a `!.lmd/auth/...` exception).

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Multi-server example (delete the single-server sections above and use
# this shape instead if you have a monorepo / multiple frontends / split
# frontend + backend):
#
# ## Test Servers
#
# - lms:         http://localhost:5173 (npm run dev --workspace=lms)
# - crm:         http://localhost:5174 (npm run dev --workspace=crm)
# - lms-backend: http://localhost:3000
#
# ## Test Auth
#
# - lms:                                   # password method (same-origin form)
#     Login URL: http://localhost:5173/login
#     Users:
#       - default: test@lms.com / testpass
#       - admin:   admin@lms.com / adminpass
# - crm:                                   # storageState method (SSO behind external IdP)
#     Method: storageState
#     Storage states:
#       - default: .lmd/auth/crm-default.json
#       - admin:   .lmd/auth/crm-admin.json
#
# Each server picks its own method independently — mix password and
# storageState freely.
#
# Tester maps a task's Scope (lms / lms-auth / crm / lms + crm) to the
# right named server by closest prefix match. Reference auth profiles as
# `<server>.<profile>` from /lmd:explore --auth.
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gitignore policy is up to you:
#
#   - COMMIT the file when test creds are shared across the team (test
#     users in a shared dev DB). Add an exception to .gitignore:
#       .lmd/
#       !.lmd/test-env.md
#
#   - GITIGNORE the file when creds are per-developer or sensitive. The
#     default .gitignore recommendation (`.lmd/`) already keeps it local;
#     each dev creates their own copy from this template.
